azgor wrote:
> This is a collection of YANIs by users of the #nethack IRC channel on
> freenode.net. Please excuse any old ideas that may be present.
>
> [1]: Silver golems: Just like gold golems, except it's silver. When
> killed, it crumbles into various types of silver equipment. Undead
> avoid them like the plague... or whatever the equivilant of the plague
> is to undead. --TheShrewdDude_
>
Then wouldn't gold golems have to crumble into gold weapons etc?  Pass.

> [2]: The Bank of Yendor: It's where you go to deposit your cash. And to
> withdraw checks and bank notes and whatnot. It's not exactly like Fort
> Ludios, because the Bank is guarded by shopkeepers. Or bankkeepers.
> --TheShrewdDude_
>
Part of the challenge of NetHack is figuring out what not to carry.
You can already store credit in shops.  I don't see the point of having
a bank.  Would there be an ATM on the astral plane?  No thanks.

> [3]: Swimming skill: Who doesn't want to have a quick refreshing dip
> once in a while? At unskilled, you'll have to skinny-dip to avoid
> drowning, but by basic you can manage to take some weight with you,
> though it might be soaked. At skilled or above, you can bathe with your
> items with impunity, and by Grand Master you'll have access to all
> sorts of aquatic fun with foocubi. --TheShrewdDude_
>
There would have to be swimming in the game for there to be a skill for
it.  Nethack hasn't needed swimming yet.  Besides, you're carrying a
lot of STUFF! and most of it doesn't like water.  And there are nasties
in any body of water you would be able to swim in.  I don't see the
need.

> [4]: when #offering, your god should sometimes grant a wish or a
> (blessed?) genocide in place of an artifact --teabing_
>
Sure.  I like it.

> [5]: Zapless condcut. No wands. --SomeoneElseYes
>
The most important wands in the game don't need to be zapped to be used
(fire/lightning/wishing).  There are already a lot of conducts.  And it
is already close enough to impossible to do some/all of them.  Is your
goal to make it actually impossible to ascend with all possible
conducts?

> [6]: Archaeologists should have some sort of bonues against rolling
> boulder traps (Indiana Jones) --ilya86
>
They certainly should if they don't already...  but I'll add that
Indiana Jones was an exceptional example of his profession.  You'll
recall in "Raiders" that he came across the body of his closest
contemporary in the first scene: "He was good.  Real good."  Sounds
like they shouldn't all get bonuses for every cinematic death trap.
But I like this one anyway.

> [7]: kicking a wall should be insta-death by falling boulders and rocks
> --shambler
>
Stand up.  Walk over to the nearest wall.  Kick it.  Did the ceiling
collapse on you and kill you?  Next.

> [8]: Items should retain their appearance when identified. (ie. i - a
> shiny ring of free action; Q - a mottled spellbook of stone to flesh)
> --TheShrewdDude
>
This would lead to some very long item names.  "Q - a blessed greased
mottled spellbook of stone to flesh called read twice"  And what about
items that have very different appearances when identified?  r - a
blessed greased thoroughly rusty +2 beaked polearm / bec-de-corbin
called newtbane"  We already have a way to list item appearances.  I
don't think the need comes up often enought to warrant this.

> [9]: In real life, it's dangerous to use a tinning kit if you don't
> know what you're doing. This should apply to Nethack as well. There
> should be a 1/100 change of dying of botulism poisoning after eating a
> self-made tin. --ijon
>
Maybe a tinning skill?  Why don't we just say that the kit came with
foolproof instructions printed on the lid.

> [10]: On occasion, a random event might occur. The dungeon might flood,
> or a freak storm might start. There might be a gas leak, or maybe a
> volcano erupts. Who knows? --TheShrewdDude
>
1. NetHack takes place indoors.  2. NetHack takes place indoors.  3.
There are no stoves or water heaters in NetHack.  4. Volcanos are
generally FREAKING HUGE with lava flows that are MILES long.  There is
already enough lava in the game for me.  Thanks, but no thanks.

> [11]: The current dungeons are so terribly boring. Why not have
> -random- levels where there might be a sea (as opposed to checkerboard
> pools), a chasm full of lava, a storm cloud, or an ice area?
> --TheShrewdDude
>
I agree that having maze after maze after maze is quite boring.  I
could imagine having a list of several dozen special levels, of which
you will find a random subsection in your game.  That might spice
things up a little.

> [12]: There should also be areas that are date-related. For example,
> one might expect a snow area in wintertime, and a tree grove in spring.
> --TheShrewdDude
>
We've been over this before.  NetHack takes place indoors.  Also,
wouldn't all of the levels have to be frozen over in winter time?  Ice
skating to Medusa's Isle just doesn't do it for me.

> [13]: Credit to n54 and Xarann: New conduct: The Counter-Pacifist - You
> must cause conflict throughout the whole game. --TheShrewdDude
>
This is impossible if you don't start with the ring.  If you do, and
you wear it for an entire game, then just post about it here.  You'll
get as much credit for it as you would if the conduct was coded into
the game.  Too rare to be worth bloating the code. ;-)

> [14]: miser conduct: you may not use gold or interact with shopkeepers
> in any way (except as a projectile) --teabing
>
You've been using gold?  I think most folks steal most everything they
need already.  Do we really need a conduct for playing the game
normally?

> [15]: Gold golems, or any golem, should eat their constituent parts.
> When doing so, they heal fully and gain 50% more hitpoints, as with the
> endgame Riders' effects. --TheShrewdDude
>
Wouldn't they just slap the material onto themselves instead?  Thank
you, but I don't thnk you should get to have a pet gold golem with
900,000,000 HP.

> [16]: When wizards die, Death should visit them. (think diskworld)
> --Botje
>
Doesn't really serve much practical purpose, does it?  Do you really
care what happens to you after you're dealt lethal damage and before
DYWYPI?

> [17]: Critical hit: Certain monsters and weapons can have special
> critical hit abilities. You might lose an arm and be unable to wear a
> shield or twoweapon, get your head bashed in and suffer a concussion,
> or get paralyzed from a blow to the spine. Of course, there should be
> magical and conventional remedies for these maladies, so that it isn't
> game-wrecking when you become a... --TheShrewdDude
>
Injuries would essentially be fatal.  Imagine if you broke a leg and
had no way to immediately cure it.  You'd be killed by the next newt
